Import "../sources/Euthyphro-Jowett.lingua"
Import "Crito.lingua"

Euthyphro: definitions, reasons, and approval (@greek-euthyphro: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @greek-socratic-inquiry, #source @greek-euthyphro-text, #philosophy @greek-crito, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-crito) { r_2B4XJJ3N8YSZ35JXZCV51QTRBD
**Read:** [Euthyphro, translated by Benjamin Jowett](../sources/Euthyphro-Jowett.lingua#r_1EH96KVFJX5TD94W0N1A3AYNZ6). Start with the demand for an account of piety and the successive answers through the discussion of what all the gods love. Read the complete short dialogue, including the later attempt to connect piety with justice. The initial investigation is approximately 5d–11b in Stephanus references.

**Preparation:** a claim and a reason from [the reading method](../foundations/Read-an-argument.lingua#r_4GMQCKPBF9BCSKDK0KPN013EBN). [Apology](Apology.lingua#r_0DWAPFJNVYT6WNTRT46ZVK82X4) is useful context for the charges surrounding Socrates, rather than a prerequisite for following the exchange.

An example of a pious action and an account of what makes any action pious do different work. Test the proposed account against its intended cases. When disagreement among gods challenges one answer, notice the revised answer: what all gods love. The next difficulty therefore requires a new argument.

The question about being loved and being pious distinguishes a thing's status from a response to it. Reconstruct why Socrates asks whether divine love follows piety or piety follows divine love. Do not assume that formulating the dilemma has settled every possible theory of religion or morality.

**Practice:** make a table with Euthyphro's proposed answer, Socrates' objection, the concession, and the next proposal. Choose a definition of fairness from everyday life and test it with one ordinary case and one difficult case.

**Check:** explain the difference between giving an example and giving a definition. Then reconstruct the approval argument and propose the strongest reply you can. State what remains unresolved at the end.

**Bridge:** definitions give reasons a clearer object. [Crito](Crito.lingua#r_54N10WGKCSCKW6XM202PCG7ZDH) asks whether reasons can guide a difficult action; [Meno](Meno.lingua#r_6NC59NWAQ6A7YZYBJ3CG12SCTN) will ask how such an investigation can teach us anything.

**Next:** [Crito](Crito.lingua#r_54N10WGKCSCKW6XM202PCG7ZDH). **Return:** [First reading path](Socratic-inquiry.lingua#r_24EDXVJN0ACP2G204JK3KCHW79) · [Ancient Greek philosophy](../Ancient-Greek-Philosophy.lingua#r_464ZHR7ZYC234A0XX2QEW47VB3).
